  "live_records": {
    "q-31972": {
      "dom_id": "step-q31972",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$9{b}^{2}+24b+16=$",
      "worked_solution": "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}=(x+y)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n9{b}^{2}+24b+16.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3b)^{2}+24b+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3b)^{2}+2(12b)+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(12b)$ as a product of $3b$ and $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3b)^{2}+2(3b\\cdot 4)+(4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of a sum formula with $x=3b$ and $y=4.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3b+4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
