    "q-111626": {
      "dom_id": "step-q111626",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Factor  $9{p}^{2}+30p+25.$",
      "worked_solution": "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2}=(a+b)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n9{p}^{2}+30p+25.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3p)^{2}+30p+(5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3p)^{2}+2(15p)+(5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(15p)$ as a product of $3p$ and $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3p)^{2}+2(3p\\cdot 5)+(5)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of a sum formula with $a=3p$ and $b=5.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3p+5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
