    "q-3009": {
      "dom_id": "step-q3009",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Factor $25{y}^{2}+50y+25.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $25.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $25,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{y}^{2}+50y+25 & = \\\\ 25({y}^{2}+2y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the square of a sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y+1 & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y+{1}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot y+{1}^{2} & = \\\\ (y+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25({y}^{2}+2y+1) & = \\\\ 25(y+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-3009-a",
