    "q-111652": {
      "dom_id": "step-q111652",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Factor $-16{k}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}k-\\frac{1}{9}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16{k}^{2}+\\frac{8}{3}k-\\frac{1}{9} & = \\\\ -(16{k}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+\\frac{1}{9})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference  formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{k}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+\\frac{1}{9} & = \\\\ (4k)^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & = \\\\ (4k)^{2}-2\\cdot (4k\\cdot \\frac{1}{3})+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(4k-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(16{k}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}k+\\frac{1}{9}) & = \\\\ -{(4k-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
