    "q-79011": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79011",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Factor $-4{x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25} & = \\\\ -(4{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+\\frac{1}{25})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+\\frac{1}{25} & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}+2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 2x)+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(2x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(4{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{5}x+\\frac{1}{25}) & = \\\\ -{(2x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
